#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a6/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43baf2bf-555a-5a7d-80d6-1e80998846ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238642} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4bf5ea-95a4-51a4-8a22-9d7407ee588f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239118} {:answer/id #uuid "a87502b6-a974-55ef-9731-5c805fa819cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} {:answer/id #uuid "a900b4a3-b49d-54e8-8cd3-b7ee4fef1cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239531} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a791a8-6243-5cbb-8e82-29b536b3f82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87502b6-a974-55ef-9731-5c805fa819cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefc758a-5b74-5f60-8c85-a8cfa0ef9113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "face32c2-838a-5a8c-a2dc-dfdf5610f42d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106399" :question/problem "![](images/49/494e360a4c22d96feb811de6ee507996a11be65ee346d837debcb927c0d28434.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,2)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c85e08-cf6a-5307-80f4-ce01ca0ebcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238422} {:answer/id #uuid "45c84b8d-f295-5003-a523-bb16c6bcf8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238658} {:answer/id #uuid "689800b2-34c6-5ad7-a7b0-9045f353fb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238964} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4de9d6-48b4-5098-a575-119212df9e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239570} {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778} :answer-field/id #uuid "45dde714-85df-57b8-888c-f62cc7cc39a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" :question/math-academy-id "q-106407" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327811" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a18201-8079-51dd-b682-32f608606117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345389} {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} {:answer/id #uuid "56f5788a-a5a6-5e32-946c-bf55db053ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346091} {:answer/id #uuid "8855c7fb-4098-5cd5-b05a-21eccba165f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346533} {:answer/id #uuid "b8010337-6cef-584f-b257-a06f6c10ce39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea18173-d655-51a6-a7a1-312a50186ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3651a5-52a0-5b3e-bfe6-600c2b9ae816" :question/math-academy-id "q-79275" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe1a9cf99e5a3b2cae80a4bfd01cdcabdc77560ccbb17ff9c7aa580a532d114.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5d/5d615d9fd29a09345b05ee4d80ffdc49198924d0cd44edb9c815270543fae60f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200dc369-b95f-5003-9958-273f823a2f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345635} {:answer/id #uuid "6a200e6f-b5ac-52f6-972d-b2490377dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346271} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c6e0b-35eb-59a1-8aa0-be651e5193c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346461} {:answer/id #uuid "baa5e144-ff07-5209-b88f-ab79ce37dd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346972} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573} :answer-field/id #uuid "90acc913-e06e-54ed-a8a3-00b923284f02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e37c1011-13e6-56e9-875d-43a2f977ac1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79280" :question/problem "![](images/75/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cf7084-0ec3-579a-9a37-96bb54f97e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345539} {:answer/id #uuid "1f910055-d120-5957-974a-884c85c30e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345627} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd7664-0cda-5513-96b9-d73b61d9c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346032} {:answer/id #uuid "81d1d327-7a47-589f-87eb-a6542bdf2c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346486} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c7cb94-e4aa-558e-8677-1a6974d7d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=64\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd7664-0cda-5513-96b9-d73b61d9c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346032} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcacd8d-e197-5e83-9032-d75dbd8e30af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e66e328-95fb-545b-9b30-55b94d00170c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79284" :question/problem "![](images/da/dafaf384e39d336444f11ed4acea1f586058c990253e434ae148cbddebbe0abd.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(8,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} {:answer/id #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404012} {:answer/id #uuid "89052cbe-3422-56ea-9b9a-942801acf80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404013} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13ccd7-add3-5ba4-bf95-5440c51f18c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404014} {:answer/id #uuid "1c440c18-9297-5a46-a77e-a81bd6a4fec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-79292" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}]}
